  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1259 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nGusty Winds and Low Humidity This Afternoon: With clear skies, \nquickly warming surface temperatures this morning, and a compact \nshort-wave passing over the region the boundary layer has become \ndeeply mixed for this afternoon as evidenced by quickly increasing \nwind gusts of 25-30kts being observed across northern WI early this \nafternoon. Along with the winds the deep mixing has also resulted in \nRHs falling to the low to middle 30s across northern WI with a few \nlocations likely dropping into the upper 20s this afternoon. \nCombination of strong winds and low RHs is creating at least \nmoderate fire danger across parts of northern WI. Winds will quickly \ndiminish this evening after 00-02Z as the PBL stabilizes. RHs will \nalso quickly recover shortly after sunset as temperatures begin to \ncool. The compact short-wave may also kick-off a few isolated \nshowers along the WI/UP border late this afternoon/evening, however, \nwith such a dry air mass in place QPF amounts are expected to be \nminimal.\n\nThursday Night Patchy Frost Potential: Surface high pressure \nbuilding over the western Great Lakes Thursday night will create \nfavorable radiational cooling conditions beneath decoupling winds \nand clear skies. Overnight minimum temperatures will drop into the \nmid to upper 30s across north-central and far northeast Wisconsin, \nincluding Rhinelander, Land O Lakes, and Crandon. NBM temperature \npercentiles show a 40 to 70 percent probability of low temperatures \nfalling to 35 degrees or colder in sandy soil areas, supporting \npatchy frost formation late Thursday night. Rapid diurnal warming \nafter sunrise Friday will quickly burn off any patchy frost with \ndaytime highs recovering into the upper 60s.\n\nWeekend Unseasonable Warmth And Strong Winds: Strong warm advection \nand return southerly flow will setup across Wisconsin Friday night \ninto the weekend as high pressure shifts to the East Coast. Inland \nmaximum temperatures Saturday and Sunday will likely reach the upper \n70s to lower 80s, approaching daily record highs at some locations. \nTightening regional pressure gradients will produce strong southerly \nwind gusts on Saturday, with NBM guidance showing a 35 to 55% \nprobability of gusts exceeding 35 mph area-wide. Medium to high \nconfidence exists in these unseasonably warm temperatures as NBM \n25th to 75th percentile spreads remain narrow at 2 to 4 degrees F \nacross most of the area, however, there is some uncertainty with how \nthe remnant cloud shield from what is currently tropical storm \nIsaias may impact daytime heating Saturday afternoon into Sunday \nmorning.\n\nAn advancing upper-level trough and associated surface cold front \nwill cross Wisconsin Sunday night into Monday, bringing returning \nrain shower chances. Limited moisture transport ahead of the \nboundary will restrict precipitation coverage, keeping 24-hour \nrainfall probabilities between 40 and 60 percent across north-\ncentral and northeast Wisconsin. NBM probabilistic guidance \nindicates a 15-30 percent probability of 24-hour rainfall exceeding \n0.50 inches, with expected liquid accumulations remaining around \n0.25 inches. Post-frontal cold advection Monday night into Tuesday \nwill drop daytime highs back into the upper 50s and lower 60s for \nearly next week.",
